WEBVTT

00:00:00.148 --> 00:00:04.540
A useful GPU cost model looks beyond the visible price of a compute instance.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.005
The workload may consume GPU capacity, attached CPU and memory, local disks,

00:00:09.005 --> 00:00:13.570
remote volumes, object storage, snapshots, network transfer, managed orchestration,

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.055
logging, monitoring, and support time from engineers and operators.

00:00:17.055 --> 00:00:19.080
Price and effective cost are not the same thing.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.770
A lower-priced resource that sits idle, waits on storage, fails repeatedly,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.740
or requires manual recovery may be more expensive than a better-matched resource that

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.150
completes reliably.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.995
Effective cost asks how much useful output the team receives for the money spent.

00:00:31.995 --> 00:00:33.505
Capacity choices also have tradeoffs.

00:00:33.505 --> 00:00:35.482
On-demand usage can be simple and flexible.

00:00:35.482 --> 00:00:39.440
Reserved or committed capacity can help predictable demand but may create waste if the

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:40.830
workload changes.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:44.800
Interruptible or preemptible capacity can reduce spend when jobs can tolerate stops and

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:45.320
restarts,

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.486
but it needs checkpointing and retry design.

00:00:47.486 --> 00:00:49.480
Hidden costs often live around the job.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.019
Large datasets may be copied repeatedly.

00:00:52.019 --> 00:00:53.110
Checkpoints may accumulate.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:55.640
Logs and metrics may grow without retention rules.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.400
Artifacts from old experiments may remain long after the decision they supported.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.320
Operational labor should be part of the picture.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.010
If a workload requires frequent manual cleanup, investigation, reruns,

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.745
or emergency budget review, that effort has cost.

00:01:07.745 --> 00:01:11.995
A good model explains both what the platform charges and how well the workload uses what

00:01:11.995 --> 00:01:12.990
it allocates.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.760
It also helps teams compare alternatives without pretending that the listed compute rate

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:17.620
is the whole story.
